  <h2>Buying Guide: Key Technical Metrics to Check Under ₹50,000</h2>
  <h3>1. 4K Sensor Crop Factor</h3>
  <p>Many entry-level camera processors cannot read out the full sensor width quickly enough in 4K resolution, forcing an artificial electronic crop (such as the 1.6x additional crop on the Canon EOS R100). This drastically shrinks your wide-angle view, turning standard lenses into medium telephoto focal lengths. Always check the official specification sheet for terms like "4K sensor crop."</p>
  <h3>2. External Audio Microphone Input</h3>
  <p>Never rely solely on an internal built-in camera microphone for corporate projects, client interviews, or YouTube content. Having a dedicated 3.5mm external audio input jack is a non-negotiable requirement for professional filmmaking and sound clarity.</p>
  <h3>3. Bit Depth and Chroma Subsampling Limitations</h3>
  <p>Every camera under ₹50,000 in India currently records video internally using an 8-bit 4:2:0 compression schema. This means your files contain fewer overall color shades compared to 10-bit cinema cameras, increasing the likelihood of color banding if you attempt harsh exposure manipulations. Therefore, practicing exact on-set white balance and exposure accuracy is critical in this tier.</p>
  <h3>4. Log vs. Flat Picture Profiles</h3>
  <p>Of the top three contenders, only the Sony ZV-1F provides legitimate wide-gamut Log recording (S-Log2 / S-Log3). The Canon EOS R100 and Panasonic Lumix G7 rely on standardized flat or "Cine" tone styles. While these do not capture as much dynamic range as true Log curves, they still afford much greater post-production latitude than standard consumer color saturation presets.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Can you actually produce professional filmmaking content with a camera under ₹50,000?</h3>
    <p>Yes, absolutely. Modern cameras in this bracket deliver crisp 4K video resolution, manual shutter speed and ISO exposure controls, and external microphone connectivity. By mastering professional lighting principles and clean audio recording, you can effortlessly generate broadcast-worthy footage.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Is a DSLR or Mirrorless camera better for a beginner filmmaker in 2026?</h3>
    <p>In 2026, mirrorless systems represent the undisputed winner for video production. They provide superior real-time electronic viewfinder previews, noticeably quieter electronic shutters, faster sensor-plane autofocus systems, more lightweight camera body structures, and forward-looking lens compatibility.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Do budget cameras under ₹50,000 feature built-in Log profiles for DaVinci Resolve grading?</h3>
    <p>Within this exact price boundary in India, only the Sony ZV-1F officially provides full S-Log2 and S-Log3 recording capabilities. The Canon EOS R100 and Panasonic Lumix G7 offer customizable flat and cinema-style picture profiles (such as Cinelike D) to aid in color flexibility.</p>
  </div>

